 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_K9H4Mmtfp5yh8ctX",
   "name": "Complex Numbers – Basics",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "894450053"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Introduction to complex numbers"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "Complex numbers are a set of numbers which contain both a real part and an imaginary part. The set of complex numbers is denoted as $ℂ$."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is an imaginary number?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Up until now, when we have encountered an equation such as $x^{2}=-1$ we would have stated that there are “no real solutions” as the solutions are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{-1}$ which are not real numbers\n- To solve this issue, mathematicians have defined one of the square roots of negative one as $i$; an imaginary number\n\n  - $\\sqrt{-1}=i$\n  - $i^{2}=-1$\n- We can use the rules for manipulating surds to manipulate imaginary numbers.\n- We can do this by rewriting surds to be a multiple of $\\sqrt{-1}$ using the fact that $\\sqrt{ab}=\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{b}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a complex number?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Complex numbers have both a real part and an imaginary part\n\n  - For example: $3+4i$\n  - The real part is 3 and the imaginary part is 4\n  \n    - Note that the imaginary part does not include the '$i$'\n- Complex numbers are often denoted by $z$ and we can refer to the real and imaginary parts respectively using $\\mathrm{Re}(z)$and $\\mathrm{Im}(z)$\n- In general:\n\n  - $z=a+bi$ This is the Cartesian form of *z*\n  - $Re ( z ) = a$\n  - $\\text{Im} ( z ) = b$\n- It is important to note that two complex numbers are equal if, and only if, both the real and imaginary parts are identical.\n\n  - For example, $3+2i$ and $3+3i$ are **not equal**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![8-1-1-complex-numbers-basic-we-solution](assets/b90cbdca6819-8-1-1-complex-numbers-basic-we-solution.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Be careful in your notation of complex and imaginary numbers.\n- For example:\n$( 3 \\sqrt{5} ) i$ could also be written as $3i\\sqrt{5}$, but if you wrote $3\\sqrt{5}i$ this could easily be confused with  $3\\sqrt{5i}$."
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_bZGdc2Hcy257xTX7",
   "name": "Complex Numbers – Basic Operations",
   "definition": ""
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Operations with complex numbers"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I add and subtract complex numbers?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When adding and subtracting complex numbers, simplify the real and imaginary parts separately\n\n  - Just like you would when collecting like terms in algebra and surds, or dealing with different components in vectors\n  - $( a + b \\mathrm{i} ) + ( c + d \\mathrm{i} ) = ( a + c ) + ( b + d ) \\mathrm{i}$\n- Complex numbers can also be multiplied by a constant in the same way as algebraic expressions:\n\n  - $k ( a + b \\mathrm{i} ) = k a + k b \\mathrm{i}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I multiply complex numbers?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The most important thing to bear in mind when multiplying complex numbers is that $i^{2}=-1$\n- We can still apply our usual rules for multiplying algebraic terms:\n\n  - $a(b+c)=ab+ac$\n  - $( a + b ) ( c + d ) = a c + a d + b c + b d$\n- Sometimes when a question describes multiple complex numbers, the notation $z_{1},z_{2},\\dots$ is used to represent each complex number"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the powers of i?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Because $i^{2}=-1$ this can lead to some interesting results for higher powers of i\n\n  - $i^{3}=i^{2}\\timesi=-i$\n  - $i^{4} = ( i^{2} )^{2} = ( - 1 )^{2} = 1$\n  - $i^{5}=(i^{2})^{2}\\timesi=i$\n  - $i^{6} = ( i^{2} )^{3} = ( - 1 )^{3} = - 1$\n- We can use this same approach of using i<sup>2</sup> to deal with much higher powers\n\n  - $i^{23} = ( i^{2} )^{11} \\times i = ( - 1 )^{11} \\times i = - i$\n  - Just remember that -1 raised to an even power is 1 and raised to an odd power is -1"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
